      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Book of Han">
      <data key="d0">Book of Han</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'Book of Han' by Ban Gu extends the biographical structure established by Sima Qian, becoming a standard format in historical writing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3920bf7b7788b93ba65dcf473dd05164</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Book of the Later Han">
      <data key="d0">Book of the Later Han</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'Book of the Later Han' is a historical document by Fan Ye that continues the biographical tradition and covers significant figures of the Later Han.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3920bf7b7788b93ba65dcf473dd05164</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Records of the Three Kingdoms">
      <data key="d0">Records of the Three Kingdoms</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'Records of the Three Kingdoms' by Chen Shou recounts the major characters and events during the Three Kingdoms period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3920bf7b7788b93ba65dcf473dd05164</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tongjian Jishi">
      <data key="d0">Tongjian Jishi</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'Tongjian Jishi' by Yuan Shu employs an event-centric style to annotate the 'Zizhi Tongjian', aiding reader comprehension.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3920bf7b7788b93ba65dcf473dd05164</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
